==========================================
NETWORKING DAY 04 — NMAP PART 1
Host Discovery & Basic Scans
==========================================

--- CHECK INSTALL ---
which nmap
nmap --version

--- FINDING YOUR OWN NETWORK RANGE ---
ip route | grep -v default          # shows your subnet, e.g. 192.168.80.0/24

--- HOST DISCOVERY (find what's alive, no port scan) ---
sudo nmap -sn 192.168.1.0/24        # ping-scan an entire subnet
sudo nmap -sn 192.168.1.1-50        # ping-scan a specific IP range
sudo nmap -sn 192.168.1.10 192.168.1.20   # ping-scan specific individual IPs

--- BASIC PORT SCANS ---
nmap <target>                        # default: top 1000 most common TCP ports
nmap -F <target>                      # FAST scan: top 100 ports only
nmap -p 22 <target>                    # scan ONE specific port
nmap -p 22,80,443 <target>              # scan a LIST of specific ports
nmap -p 1-100 <target>                   # scan a RANGE of ports
nmap -p- <target>                         # scan ALL 65535 ports (slow)

--- SAFE PRACTICE TARGETS ---
localhost / 127.0.0.1                 # your own machine, always safe
scanme.nmap.org                        # nmap project's official, publicly permitted target
<your-own-local-subnet>                 # your own network only, e.g. 192.168.x.0/24

--- READING THE OUTPUT ---
PORT      STATE   SERVICE
22/tcp    open    ssh
80/tcp    closed  http
53/tcp    filtered domain

open      -> something is actively listening and responded
closed    -> port reachable, but nothing is listening on it
filtered  -> nmap couldn't tell; a firewall is likely blocking the probe

